# Surface Chemistry and Catalysis: Where Reactions Really Happen

> Chemistry at surfaces obeys different rules from chemistry in bulk.
> A catalyst speed‑shapes a reaction without being consumed, a colloid
> refuses to settle for years, and a few grams of activated charcoal
> clean a whole tank of water — all because surface area, not amount,
> is the currency that matters.

## 1. Adsorption: The Surface Phenomenon

[definition]
**Adsorption** is the accumulation of a substance (the **adsorbate**)
on the **surface** of a solid or liquid (the **adsorbent**). It is
distinct from **absorption**, where the substance penetrates into the
bulk. When both occur together, the word is **sorption**.
[/definition]

| Contrast | Adsorption | Absorption |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Where | surface only | throughout the bulk |
| Classic example | water vapour on silica gel | water by anhydrous CaCl₂ |
| Rate | rapid initially, then slows | uniform throughout |
| Concentration | higher at surface than bulk | same everywhere |

Adsorption is always **exothermic** (ΔH < 0): gas molecules lose
freedom when they stick, so entropy decreases and enthalpy must fall
to keep the process spontaneous.

## 2. Physisorption versus Chemisorption

| Property | Physisorption | Chemisorption |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Forces | van der Waals | chemical bonds |
| Enthalpy | ~20–40 kJ/mol | ~80–240 kJ/mol |
| Reversible | yes | largely irreversible |
| Specificity | low — any gas on any solid | high — depends on reactants |
| Temperature | low temperature favours it | high temperature needed |
| Layers | multimolecular | unimolecular |

[important]
The same gas can switch mechanism: hydrogen on glass beads is
physisorbed at low temperature but **chemisorbed on nickel** at higher
temperature, forming surface hydride-like species. Temperature is the
lever that moves a system from physical to chemical adsorption — the
reason catalysts are heated before use (activation).
[/important]

## 3. Freundlich's Adsorption Isotherm

At constant temperature, the amount $x/m$ (mass adsorbed per gram of
adsorbent) grows with pressure (gas) or concentration (solution):

@math label="eq:freundlich}
\frac{x}{m} = k\,P^{1/n}, \qquad n > 1
@/math

Taking logarithms gives a straight line when
$\log(x/m)$ is plotted against $\log P$ — slope $1/n$, intercept
$\log k$. The isotherm is empirical: it works at moderate pressures
but fails at very low pressures (where $x/m \propto P$) and very high
pressures (where $x/m$ saturates).

**Worked example.** Charcoal adsorbs 0.3 g of a dye from 100 mL of
solution (mass of charcoal 1.5 g). Here $x/m = 0.3/1.5 = 0.2$ g per
gram — doubling the charcoal at the same concentration roughly
doubles the dye removed, the everyday logic of adsorption
calculations.

## 4. Catalysis: Speed Without Consumption

[definition]
A **catalyst** is a substance that changes the rate of a reaction by
offering an alternative path of lower activation energy, emerging
chemically unchanged. It cannot make a thermodynamically impossible
reaction occur, and it does not shift the position of equilibrium —
it only helps equilibrium arrive sooner.
[/definition]

| Type | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Homogeneous | acid-catalysed ester hydrolysis; NO in oxidation of SO₂ |
| Heterogeneous | Fe in Haber process; V₂O₅ in Contact process; Ni in hydrogenation |
| Enzymatic | invertase hydrolysing sucrose; zymase fermenting glucose |
| Negative (inhibitor) | glycerol slowing decomposition of H₂O₂ |

Key facts examiners test:

- A catalyst is **poisoned** by impurities that block active sites
  (arsenic poisoning the iron catalyst in Haber).
- **Promoters** enhance catalysts without catalysing themselves
  (Mo₂O₃ with Fe in Haber).
- Autocatalysis: a product speeds its own reaction (Mn²⁺ in
  KMnO₄ + oxalic acid titration).
- Shape-selective catalysis: zeolites (ZSM‑5) crack petroleum to
  gasoline in pores of molecular size.

## 5. Colloids: The Middle State

[definition]
A **colloid** is a dispersion in which particles of 1–1000 nm are
spread through a medium: neither a true solution (too big to dissolve)
nor a suspension (too small to settle). Examples: fog, milk, ink,
blood, butter, cheese, clouds.
[/definition]

| Type | Dispersed phase | Medium | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Aerosol | solid/liquid | gas | smoke, fog |
| Foam | gas | liquid | whipped cream |
| Emulsion | liquid | liquid | milk |
| Sol | solid | liquid | paint, ink |
| Gel | liquid | solid | jelly, cheese |

**Lyophilic** colloids (starch, gelatin — "solvent-loving") are
stable and self-forming; **lyophobic** colloids (metals, sulphides)
are unstable and need stabilisers, which is why they coagulate
readily.

## 6. The Signature Properties of Colloids

- **Brownian movement** — the zig-zag dance of particles bombarded by
  solvent molecules; it keeps colloids from settling.
- **Tyndall effect** — colloids scatter a light beam (visible path)
  because particle size is comparable to the wavelength of light;
  true solutions do not.
- **Electrophoresis** — charged particles migrate in an electric
  field, proving particles carry charge.
- **Coagulation** — adding electrolyte neutralises the charge and
  flocculates the sol.

[important]
**Hardy–Schulze rule:** the ion carrying the **opposite charge** to
the sol particles, and with the **highest charge**, coagulates most
efficiently. For a negative sol (As₂S₃), Al³⁺ > Ba²⁺ > Na⁺; for a
positive sol (Fe(OH)₃), phosphate > SO₄²⁻ > Cl⁻. Flocculation is
about charge, not quantity.
[/important]

Emulsification by soaps works the same way in reverse: the
hydrocarbon tail dissolves in the oil droplet, the charged head faces
water, and the resulting charged droplets repel one another instead
of merging — a **micelle** wrapping grease.

## 7. Applications That Appear in Exams

- **Activated charcoal** in gas masks and water purifiers (physical
  adsorption of toxic vapours).
- **Froth floatation** for sulphide ores — adsorption of collectors
  on ore particles makes them water-repellent.
- **Heterogeneous catalysis** on the surfaces of Fe, Ni, V₂O₅, Pt —
  reactant molecules adsorb, react on the surface, and desorb.
- **Chromatography** — differential adsorption on paper or alumina
  separates mixtures.
- **Catalytic converters** adsorb exhaust gases on platinum-group
  surfaces and convert CO/NOₓ to CO₂/N₂.
- **Medicine and daily life** — aluminium hydroxide gel as antacid
  (adsorbs acid), silica gel as desiccant, catalase degrading H₂O₂
  millions of times faster than uncatalysed decomposition.

## 8. Summary

Surface chemistry reduces to three sentences. Adsorption concentrates
molecules at surfaces — stronger when bonds form (chemisorption), and
quantified by Freundlich's isotherm. Catalysis is adsorption put to
work: a surface hosts reactants, lowers the activation energy, and
releases products unchanged. Colloids are systems stabilised by
surface charge, undone by electrolytes (Hardy–Schulze) and betrayed
by the Tyndall beam.
